## Key Findings

### Replication
- All Table 2 and Table 3 estimates replicated within 0.002 of published values
- Confirms computational reproducibility of Thompson et al. (2020)

### Extension
| Finding | Estimate | SE | Interpretation |
|---------|----------|-----|----------------|
| Dem Vote Share (full, quad) | 0.005 | 0.004 | No partisan effect |
| VBM × Post2018 | -0.005 | 0.008 | No differential effect |
| CA VCA Effect | 0.006 | 0.004 | No partisan impact |
| Turnout (full, quad) | 0.015 | 0.006 | Small positive effect |

### Substantive Conclusion
**Universal vote-by-mail has no meaningful impact on partisan electoral outcomes.** This null finding, first documented using 1996-2018 data, persists through the COVID-19 pandemic and into 2024. Despite dramatic expansion of mail voting and intense political debate, empirical evidence shows no partisan advantage to either party.

